# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. On 2026-08-16 the owner reported the home screen's
# Filters button doing nothing. It was not a code fault and not a stale service
# worker — it was the HTTP cache underneath both, and the mechanism is worth
# stating because nothing about it is obvious:
#
#   1. `sw.js` builds its versioned precache with `fetch()`.
#   2. A plain `fetch()` reads the browser's own HTTP cache first.
#   3. Pages served `js/*` with `max-age=14400` — four hours.
#   4. So bumping SHELL_VERSION renamed the cache, and the install then
#      REFILLED IT FROM THE PREVIOUS DEPLOY.
#   5. The READY sentinel then marked that cache fully built, so it never
#      self-healed on a later cold start.
#
# The phone ended up holding the NEW `index.html` (revalidated, `max-age=0`)
# against the OLD `app.js` — a page whose markup knows about a control its
# JavaScript has never heard of. It renders, it logs nothing, and the button
# does nothing. Reproduced end to end against the real worker with these exact
# headers before anything was changed.
#
# The fix has two halves and needs both:
#   - `sw.js` now fetches every precached asset with `cache: "reload"`, so an
#     install always goes to the network. That is the half that protects an
#     already-installed phone.
#   - This file stops the browser holding a stale copy in the first place,
#     which is what protects a FIRST visit landing between a deploy and the
#     service-worker update.
#
# Why `max-age=0, must-revalidate` and not a long cache with hashed filenames:
# there is no build step (ADR 0001), so there are no content hashes to put in a
# filename, and there never will be. Revalidation is the only correctness
# mechanism available. It costs a conditional request per asset — a 304 with no
# body — and only on a visit where the service worker is not already answering,
# which is the first visit and no other. Every subsequent visit is served from
# the precache without touching the network at all.
#
# Immutable by construction, so cache them hard: nothing else in site/ is.
/img/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Everything the service worker precaches must be revalidated, or a version
# bump can refill the new cache with the old deploy's bytes. This includes the
# data: a menu is precached the same way the shell is.
/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/data/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The worker itself. A browser that caches sw.js cannot learn that a new one
# exists — the update check fetches this URL, so a stale copy makes the whole
# update cycle a no-op. Browsers cap sw.js freshness at 24h regardless; saying
# it here removes the guesswork.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
